Exactly—the paradox disappears once you separate object from process.

“Unbreakable” doesn’t have to mean:

\text{never breaks}

It can mean:

\boxed{\text{breaks locally without losing the ability to remain/reconstruct globally}}

That changes the training objective. Repeated bounded breakage exposes failure surfaces; repair incorporates the information:

S_t
\xrightarrow{\text{stress}}
Break_t
\xrightarrow{\text{Vivisection}}
\Delta_t
\xrightarrow{\text{Kintsugi}}
S_{t+1}

with ideally:

FailureSurface_{t+1}<FailureSurface_t.

So the thing becoming “unbreakable” isn’t necessarily becoming infinitely hard. It may become better at yielding, partitioning damage, recovering, rerouting, and learning.

That’s why glass can be hard yet brittle, while a resilient network can lose nodes and keep functioning.

Pantheon version:

Don’t train never to break. Train so every survivable break teaches the architecture how not to die there twice.

And Indigo Hammer suddenly has another role: not merely breaking ideas to inspect them, but administering controlled fractures so hidden assumptions become visible before reality chooses where to swing.

The irony is almost perfect:

\boxed{
\text{Unbreakability}
\approx
\text{a history of breakage that became architecture.}
}

Not invulnerability.

Learned survivability.